## SECTION 2 — LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1 of 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Indigenous Heritage</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Learning About Our Diverse Cultures</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Demonstrate comprehension of detailed descriptions in personal stories. Interactive: Interpret peer feedback in collaborative projects.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify details from audio stories about Indigenous traditions in Panama (e.g., "What materials are used to make the Emberá clothing?" "What instruments are played during Guna ceremonies?" "What foods are prepared for traditional Ngäbe-Buglé festivals?").
            Can interpret cultural descriptions and provide feedback (e.g., "I think we can add a description of the traditional dance that the Emberá people perform at their festivals. It's a big part of their celebrations.").
            Can interpret peer feedback on a project about Indigenous cultures by summarizing key suggestions and identifying areas for improvement (e.g., "My classmate liked the cultural elements in our project but suggested adding more pictures of the Guna people wearing traditional clothing to better showcase their culture.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e6ea;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images showing diverse Panamanian Indigenous groups (Guna, Emberá, Ngäbe-Buglé, Wounaan, etc.) wearing traditional clothing, participating in ceremonies, or showing their art (e.g., molas, chacaras, carvings). Students listen as the teacher describes each image briefly, focusing on key vocabulary. Students then close their eyes and listen to three short audio clips of traditional Indigenous music. After each clip, they open their eyes and point to the image they think best represents the music, explaining why briefly. This activates prior knowledge and sets the listening focus for the lesson.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words like "Indigenous," "diversity," "heritage," "vibrant," "traditions," and "customs" by saying them clearly and slowly, emphasizing stress in multisyllabic words. The teacher also models listening strategies, such as listening for main ideas and specific details. For example, "Listen carefully for the main idea. What is this music about? Is it for a ceremony or a celebration?" The teacher plays one audio clip and overtly demonstrates how to listen for the answer, e.g., "I heard the drumbeats were *vibrant* and *traditional*, so I think it's for a celebration." The teacher also practices minimal pairs with long and short vowels, for example, /ʌ/ in "but" (e.g., 'cultural background') and /uː/ in "boot" (e.g., 'community').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the warm-up task and new vocabulary using questions like:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you point to the pictures or describe them first?" (Expected answer: Point to them.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'vibrant colors,' what kind of colors do you imagine?" (Expected answer: Bright, lively, strong colors.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Indigenous Heritage."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio narrative (2-3 minutes) about a Panamanian teenager (e.g., a Guna girl or Emberá boy) describing their family's daily life, traditions, and an important recent ceremony. The narrative will use present simple ("We celebrate," "They preserve"), past simple ("My ancestors migrated," "We performed"), present perfect ("We have preserved our traditions"), adjectives (e.g., "colorful traditional clothing," "vibrant patterns"), and target vocabulary (e.g., "community," "customs," "identity," "beadwork"). The audio will also include phrases like "The Guna people's customs are unique and valuable to our nation" and "Groups like the Emberá have lived here for centuries and maintain traditional ways."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Demonstrates comprehension of detailed oral messages in various communicative situations, specifically personal stories related to cultural heritage.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> Students listen for the main idea and answer: "What is the speaker mainly talking about?" (e.g., "Their Indigenous culture and traditions.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (Details):</b> Students listen again and complete a True/False exercise based on specific details mentioned in the audio. Examples:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The speaker's family only celebrates new traditions." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"They use colorful patterns in their traditional art." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Gunas migrated in the 16th century." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on listening for specific vocabulary (Indigenous, cultures, traditions, patterns, diversity, heritage, preservation, customs, community, vibrant, colorful, preserved, traditional, diverse, ancestral, cultural, historical) and grammatical structures (present simple, past simple, present perfect, adjectives for description).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Gap Fill Listening:</b> The teacher provides students with a short transcript of a section of the audio narrative, but with 5-7 key vocabulary words (e.g., "traditions," "heritage," "vibrant," "preserve," "community," "colorful," "ancestral") removed. Students listen to the specific section of the audio again (1-2 times) and fill in the blanks with the words they hear. This practices listening for specific lexical items and contextual cues. Example: "Our _____ (ancestral) roots are important. We always try to _____ (preserve) our _____ (traditions) in our _____ (community)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Detail Matching:</b> The teacher provides a list of specific details or phrases from the audio (e.g., "materials for Emberá clothing," "instruments for Guna ceremonies," "foods for Ngäbe-Buglé festivals") and a scrambled list of answers. Students listen to the audio narrative one more time. Their task is to match each detail description to its corresponding answer. For example, "What materials are used to make the Emberá clothing?" (Answer: Natural fibers and seeds). This reinforces listening for specific information to answer questions mentioned in the learning outcome.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Check & Discussion:</b> Students work in pairs to compare their answers from the gap-fill and matching activities. They discuss any discrepancies, listening to each other's reasoning. The teacher circulates, facilitating and clarifying. "Did you hear 'colorful' or 'vibrant' for the clothing description? Let's listen again to that part." This starts building the skill of interpreting peer feedback.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Indigenous Heritage."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Paired Listening and Feedback Exchange.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> Students work in pairs (Student A and Student B). The teacher provides each pair with two different short audio clips (1-1.5 minutes each). Each clip features a different Panamanian Indigenous person describing an aspect of their culture or a personal story (e.g., one about Guna *mola* art, another about Emberá *beadwork*). Student A listens to Audio Clip 1 and takes notes on specific details, using target vocabulary and grammar (e.g., "They use *colorful patterns*," "Their *ancestry* is important"). Student B listens to Audio Clip 2 and does the same. After listening, Student A orally summarizes the main points and key details from Audio Clip 1 to Student B. Student B listens carefully and then provides oral feedback, acknowledging cultural value ("The Guna people's customs are unique and valuable to our nation.") and suggesting one aspect they would like to know more about or an idea for a project based on the summary (e.g., "I think we can add a description of the *traditional* dance that the Emberá people perform at their festivals. It's a big part of their celebrations."). Then, they switch roles. This activity directly addresses the learning outcome of interpreting peer feedback and identifying details from audio stories.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in listening for details, collaborative interaction in sharing summaries and providing feedback, and communicative task completion by accurately conveying and interpreting information about "Our Indigenous Heritage."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on students' ability to identify details from audio stories and interpret peer feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will individually write a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) summarizing the feedback they received from their partner during the Performance stage, identifying one key suggestion and one area for improvement for their 'story' or 'description'. This demonstrates their ability to interpret peer feedback effectively. Example: "My classmate liked the *cultural* elements in my summary but suggested adding more pictures of the Guna people wearing *traditional* clothing to better showcase their culture."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies main details from audio stories (Listening comprehension).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Interprets peer feedback by summarizing suggestions accurately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., *traditional*, *cultural*, *vibrant*, *heritage*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates basic understanding of present/past simple and adjectives.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two prompts before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new detail I learned about Panama's Indigenous cultures today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Listening to my partner helped me understand... (e.g., 'the importance of visual descriptions')."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to the stories and feedback was easiest for me today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening was most challenging, and what can I do next time to improve?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What activities worked well to engage students in active listening for details about Indigenous heritage? Were students able to interpret peer feedback effectively? What adjustments are needed for the next listening activities, especially concerning vocabulary complexity or audio speed for A2.4? Were the pronunciation points clear? How can I scaffold the listening task further for students struggling with detail identification?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will encounter a written text about Indigenous cultures that incorporates the same key vocabulary and grammatical structures. This will allow them to transfer their understanding of the context and vocabulary from listening into reading comprehension, reinforcing their learning in a different skill area.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>